A||||4||Fformula88||Do you quote magazine test numbers?||||1||
Z||000000||Fformula88||12-04-2001||10:22 AM||||I know it is very fashionable to compare the performance of cars based on the magazine test numbers around this forum.  I am very tired of these numbers starting flame wars, especially when someone gives their Fiero's track time at say 7.7 sec 0-60 and the next guy says, "but such and such tested a (fill in the car) and it ran a 7.3 do your car is slow and sucks dude".  <P>In a nut shell, they are a good basis but are not necessarily the standard for a particular car.  There are so many factors in car performance that a 0-60 or 1/4 mile time can very a lot.  Weather and atmospheric conditions, engine age/milage, driver skill, road surface, tire condition etc etc.  Unless the cars are tested at the same venue on the same day relatively close to the same time, then forget these comparisons as they are not a true indicator.<P>Example... Motor Trend initially tested a Celica GTS' 0-60 at 6.6 seconds.  Their lates test (a comparo) had it at 7.2.  Thats a .6 difference for the same car!  Their testers are really god at launching and driving (thats their jobs!) so I wouldn't think driver skill is the big difference here.  Maybe another 500 feet in elevation it would have run a 7.5?  Or if the temperature was 90 degrees?  <P>The point... if your going to compare all these times, and they are within a second of each other (especially within half a second!) , one may be faster than the other but it is also may not be!<P>Sorry for the rant, but I am tired of bickering that goes on over 0-60 times that are .2 seconds apart.<BR><P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/formulasig.JPG">           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">    <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000001||LoW_KeY||12-04-2001||10:31 AM||||Oh yeah.. I see stats like that all the time Chevy announced on their site that the Z06 ran 12 flat and 4 sec 0-60 in magazines they were a full 1 - 1.5 seconds off.  <P>they were getting 13.5 in the 1/4 and 5.3 0-60, thats quite a bit difference on that case..<P>But I see where your coming from with the 7.3 vs the 7.7.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: FierFormula88   <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/kcbanner.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Master_Sushi||12-04-2001||01:17 PM||||There are so many possible differences during car road tests. One I have always though about is weight of the test driver. Now what if one magazine tested with a 130lbs guy driving the car, then some other magazine uses a guy that weighs like 300lbs?? That’s a HUGE difference. When someone else is in my car with me, I can tell its allot slower. As said above track conditions are a huge factor.  Its just little things like this that makes tests not completely accurate.<BR>The way Fieros seem to sometimes be underrated, you think they had some 350lbs slob testing them in the snow.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Ok, maybe not that bad   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">..<P>edit.. typo<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 12-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||12-04-2001||02:52 PM||||Not only does driver, track conditions, elevation, and temperature make a difference, But so does the car! I think it's the tolerences of mass production, but some cars are faster then an identical car.<BR>My first fiero was a stock 86 SE 4-spd. And I had no problems keeping up and passing 305 V-8 F-body cars and Mustang GT's back in 86/87. Once they F-bodies got the 350 and the Mustangs got the boost in HP then it was a different story. I have driven some stock Fiero's that seemed faster than the "magazine average" and some that were slower. Mass production of any car is going to have differences. I bet you can take 5 cars all being identical in every way and run them on a dyno and you will have 5 different readings. I would bet at least a 10hp swing if not more. <BR> If a magazine get's a car at the low end of the tolerence stack ups, it'll test the car slower. Then a different car goes to a different magazine and the tolerences are higher the car will seem faster.<P>Now start mixing all the "variable's" previously stated above and the car's inherent tolerences when made, and you never know you'll get. But I think your guess of .5 second swing would be the best way. So if a particular car is tested 7.5 seconds 0-60, You could get a quick 7.0 second car or a dog 8.0 second car, depending on the "luck of the draw"||||reg||1
Z||000004||danny boy||12-04-2001||05:31 PM||||Any time now I'm sure TRIAD will post saying everyone is just imagining things and that R&T's times are the only real times of a particular car and no one should ever believe any different. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PFF_sig_5.jpg"> ||||reg||1
